    m_Modifications:
    - target: {fileID: 3357063327598446276, guid: 6a3b6aa2e571ae041b78fd60af199e5e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_Text
      value: The Marsh Rabbit (Sylvilagus palustris) is a slightly smaller, darker
        version of the more familiar eastern cottontail rabbit, which is best known
        for its conspicuous cotton puff tail. In contrast, the marsh rabbit has a
        small gray-brown tail. It is found throughout Florida and due to being a
        strong swimmer prefers to make its home near the water. Their diet consists
        mainly of the various plants found in and around wetlands. Breeding occurs
        year-round but peaks December through June. On average, a female marsh rabbit
        produces six or seven litters of two to four young per litter each year.
        By four weeks of age, the young rabbits are weaned and are foraging for themselves.
        As nocturnal creatures their most active at dusk and night.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
